

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Noted: server-issued id from the login response.

  [1mSecond question: how long should that identity survive?[0m This one determines the storage mechanism and, with it, the
  security surface — it's the hardest of these to change later, because anything already written to a store has to be
  read back or migrated by whatever you switch to.

  - [3mIn-memory only[0m — a module holding the value for the current page load. Simplest, nothing to clear, no storage to
    leak. But a refresh loses it, so every form you add has to tolerate "not logged in" after any reload. For a
    multi-form app that's a lot of friction.
  - [3msessionStorage (per tab)[0m — survives refresh, dies when the tab closes. Matches how people expect a login to behave,
    and scopes the data to one tab so a shared machine doesn't leak identity between sessions.
  - [3mlocalStorage (across restarts)[0m — the "remember me" behavior. Survives browser restarts, which is what users often
    want, but it means a user id sits on disk until something explicitly clears it, and you now own a logout path that
    actually removes it.

  My recommendation is sessionStorage. It gives you the thing that actually blocked the in-memory option — surviving a
  refresh — without putting identity on disk indefinitely, and it keeps logout honest since closing the tab is already a
  real exit. If you later want "remember me", that's a deliberate upgrade to localStorage behind the same interface,
  not a rewrite. Worth saying plainly: a user id is not a secret the way a session token is, so none of these three is
  dangerous on its own — but whatever we pick, the interface should be the only thing that touches storage, so swapping
  it later is a one-file change.
